<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stephen Báthory</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1533">1533</a>–<a href="/years/1586">1586</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stephen Báthory was prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> from 1571 and king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and grand duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> from 1576 until his death. A gifted commander, he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV of Russia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a>, besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a>, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truce-of-yam-zapolsky">Truce of Yam-Zapolsky</a> in 1582 secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. He also founded the university at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stephen-bathory.jpg" alt="Stephen Báthory" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Martin Kober · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous_Stephen_B%C3%A1thory_(detail)_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ivan IV’s capture of Kazan in 1552 was followed by the long Livonian War of 1558–1583, which began with conquests in Livonia but ended in defeat by Poland-Lithuania under <b>Stephen Báthory</b> and by Sweden, with the loss of Russia’s gains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Near Orăștie in Transylvania, Hungarian forces under <b>Stephen Báthory</b> and Paul Kinizsi destroyed a large Ottoman raiding army.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-13">13 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Stephen Báthory</b> drove out the Russians by 1582 and reorganised Livonia into districts, promoting the Counter-Reformation through the Jesuits at Riga and Dorpat, which offended the Lutheran German nobility and towns.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-livonia">Duchy of Livonia</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1581 it accepted the rule of <b>Stephen Báthory</b> of Poland-Lithuania, and it later became the capital of Swedish Livonia and then of the Governorate of Livonia.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-city-of-riga">Free City of Riga</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Stephen Báthory</b> became king of Poland in 1576, and Gabriel Bethlen made Gyulafehérvár a centre of Protestant learning.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-transylvania">Principality of Transylvania</a></li><li><blockquote>The city became a Muscovite fortress and withstood the siege of <b>Stefan Batory</b> in 1581.</blockquote><a href="/state/pskov-republic">Pskov Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Șimleu Silvaniei, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grodno">Hrodna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.23,22.8&amp;year=1567" aria-label="Șimleu Silvaniei on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5084 506 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Stephen Báthory"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5534 776h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5534 776h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.23,22.8&amp;year=1567">What was here in 1567? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
